1.text:
The text you quote is the canonical French “disappointed visitor” opening of the post-war Heidegger – France dialogue, textually documented in the Beaufret–Heidegger *Correspondance*.

It is Jean Beaufret, 1901-1982, translator of *Sein und Zeit* and Heidegger’s first regular French guest after 1945, who writes it after his first post-war visit to Freiburg in summer/autumn 1945, in the forms usually cited as 9 July 1945 and the definitive reprise of 26 November 1945 / winter 1945-46.

In substance:

* “I believed that a man who can write *Being and Time* could understand something about men”;
* “I could not believe that metaphysics enclosed a spirit within itself, confusing intimacy with hermeticism… intimacy offers a more delicate possibility of engaging with others without losing oneself in them”;
* “After all this, I leave as sad and tormented as I arrived, with the hidden pain of having been mistaken at a decisive moment in my life… I am not leaving angry”;
* “ultimate solitude is the only way to be close to others”;
* “something has passed between the two of us that will never be erased from my being. Allow me to dispel any possible misunderstanding regarding this letter by calling you a friend. You will remain a friend to me in the secret solitude of my existence”;
* “the worth of an existence is not measured by the height of its achievements, but by the depth of its roots”.

That chagrin-and-friendship letter broke the post-war silence. Heidegger answered it in a warm conciliatory tone, 24 August 1945 / spring 1946, and in 1947-48 broke with his self-imposed isolation and invited Beaufret to come and stay with him, first in Freiburg and then at the Hütte at Todtnauberg. The visits of 1948, 1951 and 1953 open the 1945-1962 Beaufret-Heidegger dialogue which mediates the *Letter on Humanism*.

The wording is habitually reproduced in literature in the 1933-34 prototype form, i.e. under the name of Jean Wahl, 1888-1974, whose autumn 1933 Freiburg protest letter is the historical model on which Beaufret consciously modelled his 1945 letter, and in popular memory it is conflated with Jean-Paul Sartre’s winter 1933/34 visit and the 1933-36 Todtnauberg episode. Wahl/Sartre are the rhetorical prototype; the textually documented instance that is tied to an explicit invitation to Heidegger’s home/Hütte is Beaufret’s 1945-46 letter and Heidegger’s reply to it.

